<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="UTF-8"/><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width"/><title>{title} · The Evolution of Bikes</title><meta name="description" content={description}/><link rel="canonical" href={canonical}/><link rel="alternate" hreflang="da" href={danish}/><link rel="alternate" hreflang="en" href={canonical}/><link rel="alternate" hreflang="x-default" href={danish}/><meta property="og:type" content="article"/><meta property="og:locale" content="en_GB"/><meta property="og:site_name" content="The Evolution of Bikes"/><meta property="og:title" content={title}/><meta property="og:description" content={description}/><meta property="og:url" content={canonical}/><meta property="og:image" content={ogImage}/><meta property="og:image:alt" content="Fiorenzo Magni biting an inner tube during the 1956 Giro d’Italia"/><meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image"/><meta name="twitter:title" content={title}/><meta name="twitter:description" content={description}/><meta name="twitter:image" content={ogImage}/><link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com"/><link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.gstatic.com" crossorigin/><link href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=DM+Sans:wght@400;500;600&family=Libre+Caslon+Display&display=swap" rel="stylesheet"/><link rel="stylesheet" href={`${base}/styles/global.css`}/><script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify({'@context':'https://schema.org','@type':'Article',headline:title,description,inLanguage:'en',mainEntityOfPage:canonical,image:ogImage,datePublished:'2026-10-06',publisher:{'@type':'Organization',name:'The Evolution of Bikes',url:'https://teob.dk/'}})} /></head><body>
<EnglishHeader current="stories" danishPath={`/historier/${slug}/`}/><main><article class="magni-story"><header class="story-hero"><p class="section-label">Giro d’Italia · 1956</p><h1>{title}</h1><p>An injured shoulder, a length of rubber and a mechanic with an idea that only had to work as far as the next finish.</p></header>
<figure class="story-image hero"><OptimizedImage src={magniTube} alt="Fiorenzo Magni pulling an inner tube with his teeth during the 1956 Giro d’Italia" widths={[480,768,900]} sizes="(max-width: 900px) 92vw, 760px" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high"/><figcaption>Fiorenzo Magni at the 1956 Giro d’Italia with the tube between his handlebars and teeth. Photographer unknown. Wikimedia Commons, public domain in Italy.</figcaption></figure>
<div class="story-copy"><p class="lead">Fiorenzo Magni could no longer pull normally on the handlebars. Ernesto Colnago tied on an inner tube that the rider could grip between his teeth.</p><h2>The crash</h2><p>On stage 12 from Grosseto to Livorno, the 35-year-old Magni crashed on the descent from Volterra. His left shoulder was badly injured. Sources disagree on whether the first fracture was to the collarbone or shoulder blade, but its effect was clear: the arm could no longer work normally.</p><p>Magni refused to abandon. He had won the Giro three times and was riding his final professional season. The point was not to pretend that a fracture did not hurt. It was to find a way of keeping his body on the bicycle.</p><h2>Colnago’s length of rubber</h2><p>Ernesto Colnago was working as a mechanic. He later recalled cutting a length from a Clement inner tube and tying one end to the handlebars. Magni held the other end in his mouth. When the road climbed, he could pull with his neck and teeth and take some of the load away from the injured side.</p><p>It was not an extra handlebar, nor a repair to the body. Magni still had limited control and the device did nothing to remove the pain. It merely gave him one more point to pull against.</p></div>
<aside class="pull-quote"><p>“I suggested tying an inner tube to the handlebars so he could pull with his teeth.”</p><span>Ernesto Colnago’s later recollection of the Giro</span></aside>
<div class="portrait-row"><figure><OptimizedImage src={magniPortrait} alt="Fiorenzo Magni during the 1951 Tour de France" widths={[480,768,1106]} sizes="(max-width: 760px) 88vw, 430px"/><figcaption>Fiorenzo Magni during the 1951 Tour de France. Photograph: Harry Pot/Anefo, Nationaal Archief, CC BY-SA 3.0 NL. Cropped from a larger photograph.</figcaption></figure><div><h2>Another crash</h2><p>A few days later Magni hit a hole in the road and crashed again. In his own later account, he said that his upper arm was also broken. He still continued.</p><p>The Giro was overturned on the freezing stage over Monte Bondone. Charly Gaul won the race; Magni finished second. The inner tube did not bring him victory. It allowed him to remain in the race long enough to reach Milan.</p></div></div>
<section class="fact-strip" aria-label="In brief"><div><strong>35</strong><span>Magni’s age in his final professional season</span></div><div><strong>Stage 12</strong><span>where the shoulder was injured</span></div><div><strong>2nd</strong><span>Magni’s final Giro position</span></div><div><strong>1 tube</strong><span>between handlebars and teeth</span></div></section>
<section class="period-sources"><p class="section-label">Sources and image notes</p><div><ul><li><a href="https://www.rouleur.cc/blogs/the-rouleur-journal/ernesto-colnago" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Rouleur: Ernesto Colnago recalls the device</a></li><li><a href="https://www.corriere.it/cultura/11_febbraio_14/distefano-fiorenzomagni_fa2784f2-3810-11e0-9d0e-ca1b56f3890e.shtml" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Corriere della Sera: Fiorenzo Magni’s own recollection</a></li><li><a href="https://www.gazzetta.it/Speciali/Giroditalia/archivio-storico/it/edizioni/edizione_41.shtml" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">La Gazzetta dello Sport: 1956 Giro d’Italia result</a></li><li><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fiorenzo_Magni_1956.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Wikimedia Commons: the photograph from the Giro</a></li></ul><p>Sources differ on the precise first fracture. The article therefore uses the defensible wording “badly injured shoulder”.</p></div></section>
